DXWEB AND DXWEB ADMIN - INSTALLATION, UPDATE AND RECOVERY
========================================================
Operational manual in English.
Revision: November 4, 2026.

1. PURPOSE, ARCHITECTURE AND SCOPE
----------------------------------
This is the SYSOP's installation and management manual. The only
documented interactive entry point is:

  cd /spider
  perl dxweb/dxweb-menu.pl

The menu internally uses the existing transactional engine to plan,
install, check status and recover configurations. The SYSOP does NOT
need to run that engine directly. The menu does not copy or update
application code: DXWeb and DXWeb Admin come from the DXSpider Git tree.

DXWeb and DXWeb Admin are separate applications: they can be installed,
configured and managed individually or together. Turnstile protects
login in both tested versions, DXWeb 4.0.3 and DXWeb Admin 2.0.3;
DXWeb also protects public registration. This protection does not
replace passwords or SYSOP authorization.

2. FILES, REQUIREMENTS AND PREPARATION
--------------------------------------
  /spider/dxweb/dxweb-menu.pl              Sole SYSOP interface
  /spider/dxweb/dxweb-manager.pl           Existing internal engine
  /spider/dxweb/tools/turnstile-config.pl  Turnstile configuration
  /spider/INSTALL_DXWEB.en                 This manual
  /spider/dxweb/                           DXWeb application
  /spider/dxweb-admin/                     DXWeb Admin application

The dxweb/install.pl installer copies a source tree to another
destination; it is NOT the normal service configuration procedure.
The earlier diagram remains at
/spider/dxweb/docs/DXWeb_Configuration_ES.png; check the ports before
using it for a specific installation.

As root on the DXSpider server, check:

  cd /spider || exit 1
  ls -ld dxweb dxweb-admin
  perl -c dxweb/dxweb-menu.pl
  perl -c dxweb/dxweb-manager.pl
  perl -c dxweb/tools/turnstile-config.pl
  systemctl cat dxspider.service
  systemctl cat dxweb.service
  systemctl cat dxweb-admin.service

The last two units may not exist during a first installation. The
engine preserves the identity of existing services or attempts to
inherit it from the DXSpider service. The menu allows an explicit
user and group to be specified when appropriate. First check that
they exist:

  id sysop
  getent group spider

These are examples: do not assume every system uses sysop:spider.
Do not recursively change ownership or permissions under /spider.

3. MAIN MENU AND WORKFLOW ORDER
-------------------------------
Running the command in section 1 displays:

  1. DXWeb
  2. DXWeb Admin
  3. Both applications
  4. Cloudflare Turnstile
  5. Rollback
  6. Exit

Options 1, 2 and 3 provide a submenu:
  1. Service status
  2. Plan / install / update
  3. Back

Sequence for a first installation:
  A. Check prerequisites and the code present under /spider.
  B. Choose 1, 2 or 3 depending on which applications to install.
  C. Choose 2 to select access mode and scheme; review the plan.
  D. Confirm Apply ONLY if the plan is correct.
  E. Configure Turnstile keys using main menu option 4.
  F. Check services using submenu option 1 and test the websites.

For updates: first update the code using the repository's normal
procedure; open the menu, select the component, review the plan and
apply it. The menu manages configuration and services, not Git.

4. CHOOSING THE DEPLOYMENT FROM THE MENU
----------------------------------------
Under Plan / install / update, the menu asks:

  Access: 1 Direct, 2 Nginx
  Scheme: 1 HTTP, 2 HTTPS

If HTTPS is selected, it requests an existing public hostname,
certificate and private key. It validates paths and file existence
before sending the plan to the engine, which also checks certificate
validity, whether the key matches and whether the certificate covers
the hostname. It does not issue certificates.

The menu asks for the service identity:
  1. Preserve or detect existing user/group
  2. Explicitly specify user and group

The engine first performs a simulation (plan). The menu applies
changes only after explicit SYSOP confirmation. If planning fails,
it does not offer to apply. Installation may create or update services,
configure Nginx if selected and restart affected services.

4.1. FIRST INSTALLATION, DIRECT HTTP
------------------------------------
Recommended as the baseline case, without Nginx.
Select main menu option 3 (both applications),
submenu option 2,
Direct and HTTP, with detected or explicit service identity as
appropriate for the system.
Review the plan and confirm.
For a single web application, use main menu option 1 or 2.

4.2. DIRECT HTTPS
-----------------
Select main menu option 1, 2 or 3,
submenu option 2,
Direct and HTTPS.
Enter the existing DNS name, certificate and key.
TLS terminates in the applications themselves.
Verify that the certificate includes the hostname.

4.3. NGINX HTTP OR HTTPS
------------------------
This is a technical example; there is no general official support
for proxies on other servers or for URL subpaths. Before installing:

  nginx -t
  ls -la /etc/nginx/sites-enabled/
  mkdir -p /root/dxweb-nginx-backup
  cp -a /etc/nginx/nginx.conf /root/dxweb-nginx-backup/
  cp -a /etc/nginx/sites-available /root/dxweb-nginx-backup/
  cp -a /etc/nginx/sites-enabled /root/dxweb-nginx-backup/

Choose Nginx in the submenu and HTTP or HTTPS as appropriate. For
HTTPS, provide the hostname, certificate and key. The engine uses
/etc/nginx/sites-available/dxweb-installer.conf and its link in
sites-enabled, and creates proxies to internal ports 7480 and 7481.
Do not delete unrelated sites or force installation if an incompatible
site is detected (for example, an existing dxweb.conf). For a first
Nginx installation, selecting both applications is recommended. The
engine can preserve the other application block during a partial
update when it recognizes the managed configuration exactly.

4.4. UPDATES AND SINGLE-APPLICATION INSTALLATIONS
-------------------------------------------------
Select DXWeb or DXWeb Admin from the main menu.
Keep the access mode, scheme, hostname and certificates of the
working installation; review differences in the plan.
Do not mix Direct and Nginx without planning a migration.
Updating code from Git is a separate step.
Restarting DXSpider is not necessary when updating only the websites.

5. CLOUDFLARE TURNSTILE: OBTAINING AND CONFIGURING KEYS
-------------------------------------------------------
Turnstile does NOT automatically create keys.
The SYSOP must obtain them from Cloudflare. This step is required
when protection is enabled.
Access to the relevant Cloudflare account is required.

5.1. OBTAINING THE KEYS
-----------------------
  1. Open https://dash.cloudflare.com/ and sign in or create an account.
  2. Open Turnstile and create a widget (Add widget).
  3. Select Managed mode.
  4. Register the EXACT hostnames browsers will use to access DXWeb
     and DXWeb Admin. If they share a hostname but use different ports,
     register the hostname; ports are not part of the authorized name.
     If the hostnames differ, include both in the widget configuration
     where supported.
  5. For SSH tunnel tests using http://localhost:17380 and :17381,
     ensure localhost is authorized in the Turnstile configuration.
     127.0.0.1 is not equivalent to the hostname localhost.
  6. Copy the Site Key (public, used by the browser) and the Secret
     Key (private, used by the server for verification).
  7. Keep the Secret Key out of screenshots, tickets, repositories,
     logs, public files and commands that may remain in shell history.

5.2. STORING AND ENABLING KEYS THROUGH THE MENU
----------------------------------------------
Open main menu option 4 (Cloudflare Turnstile):
  1. Setup instructions
  2. Show configuration status
  3. Store / replace keys
  4. Enable Turnstile
  5. Disable Turnstile
  6. Back

Select 3 and confirm.
Enter the Site Key and Secret Key when prompted.
Secret Key input is hidden when using an interactive terminal.
Keys are stored in /etc/dxweb/turnstile.json with restrictive
permissions (0600).
The configuration is initially created DISABLED.
Then choose 4 to enable it.
Choose 2 to verify CONFIGURED and ENABLED.
The script requires root to change the configuration.

The configuration is shared by applications reading that file.
The menu does not create separate keys per application and does not
itself verify that the hostnames registered with Cloudflare are valid.
After changing keys or enabling/disabling protection, check whether
the applications need a restart to reload configuration; perform any
restart in a controlled manner, without restarting DXSpider.

5.3. ACTUAL TURNSTILE VERIFICATION
----------------------------------
Open both websites using their authorized hostnames. Check that the
widget appears and can be completed. In DXWeb, test login, logout and
another login in the same session; open a new session and verify that
another check is required. Also test public registration. In DXWeb
Admin, check CAPTCHA before login and the usual administrative
authorization (sufficient privileges); CAPTCHA does not grant privileges.

For diagnostics, check /turnstile-config.json on each server: it
should show enabled and the public site_key, NEVER secret_key.
If the widget does not appear, inspect the browser console, Cloudflare
script loading, authorized hostname and initialization errors.
If a key is incorrect, do not bypass the control: review configuration
and logs. HTTP 200 or WS 101 alone does not prove authentication works.

6. VERIFICATION AND SECURITY TESTS
----------------------------------
In the menu, choose 1/2/3 and then Service status. Also verify:

  systemctl is-active dxweb.service dxweb-admin.service
  systemctl show dxweb.service -p ActiveState -p MainPID
  systemctl show dxweb-admin.service -p ActiveState -p MainPID
  ss -lntp | grep -E ':(7380|7381|7480|7481)\b'

For direct HTTP:
  curl -f http://127.0.0.1:7380/software-version.json
  curl -f http://127.0.0.1:7381/software-versions.json

For Nginx, check nginx -t and the public URLs. Test login,
registration, WebSocket, spots, supervision and permissions.

7. RECOVERY FROM THE MENU
-------------------------
Transactions are normally stored in:
  /var/backups/dxweb-installer/

Select main menu option 5, Rollback. Enter the absolute path to the
transaction containing manifest.json. The menu first presents a
recovery plan; only then does it ask for confirmation to apply.
Do not roll back without inspecting the plan.

Rollback restores configuration and service state; it does NOT change
the Git revision of the code. When rolling back a first installation,
service units may be absent afterward. The engine creates a safety
checkpoint and uses a bounded shutdown. Do not repeat rollback
blindly. Do not delete databases, filters or certificates to recover.

8. TECHNICAL REFERENCE: MODES, PORTS AND CONFIGURATION
------------------------------------------------------
8.1. MODES AND PORTS
--------------------

  Mode                 DXWeb          Admin          DXSpider IntMsg
  -------------------  -------------- -------------- ---------------
  DIRECT HTTP          0.0.0.0:7380   0.0.0.0:7381   127.0.0.1:27754
  NGINX, HTTP backend  127.0.0.1:7480 127.0.0.1:7481 127.0.0.1:27754
  NGINX, public        port 7380      port 7381      not exposed

In direct mode, HTTPS configures TLS in the applications.
With Nginx, HTTPS configures TLS in the proxy, while the
applications continue serving HTTP on loopback.

Port 27754 is not a browser port.
The default is 127.0.0.1:27754. An update preserves DXS_HOST and
DXS_PORT from existing service units.

8.2. CONFIGURATION VARIABLES AND PORTS
--------------------------------------

The following variables are set as Environment= in systemd units.
The manager generates them according to the selected mode.
Manual editing is unnecessary when using the installer.

  Variable              Application      Purpose
  --------------------  ---------------  -----------------------------
  DXS_HOST              Both             IntMsg listener host
  DXS_PORT              Both             IntMsg listener port
  HTTP_HOST             DXWeb            HTTP listening address
  HTTP_PORT             DXWeb            HTTP listening port
  DXWEB_HOST            DXWeb Admin      Admin listening address
  DXWEB_PORT            DXWeb Admin      Admin listening port
  DXWEB_CLIENT_PORT     DXWeb Admin      DXWeb backend port
  DXWEB_CLIENT_HOST     DXWeb Admin      DXWeb connection host
  DXWEB_CLIENT_SCHEME   DXWeb Admin      DXWeb connection scheme
  DXWEB_SCHEME          Both             HTTP or HTTPS scheme
  MOJO_REVERSE_PROXY    Both             Trust HTTP reverse proxy
  DXSPIDER_ROOT         Both             DXSpider tree path
  DXWEB_TLS_CERT        Both             Direct TLS certificate
  DXWEB_TLS_KEY         Both             Direct TLS private key

Typical values for a new installation:

  Variable              Direct HTTP      With Nginx
  --------------------  ---------------  -----------------------------
  DXS_HOST              127.0.0.1        127.0.0.1
  DXS_PORT              27754            27754
  HTTP_HOST             0.0.0.0          127.0.0.1
  HTTP_PORT             7380             7480
  DXWEB_HOST            0.0.0.0          127.0.0.1
  DXWEB_PORT            7381             7481
  DXWEB_CLIENT_PORT     7380             7480
  MOJO_REVERSE_PROXY    0                1

DXS_HOST and DXS_PORT identify DXSpider's IntMsg service,
not the browser's HTTP access. During an update, the manager
preserves their values if they already appear in installed units.

HTTP_HOST and HTTP_PORT belong exclusively to DXWeb.
DXWEB_HOST and DXWEB_PORT belong to DXWeb Admin. The
DXWEB_CLIENT_PORT variable tells Admin the DXWeb backend port;
it must not be confused with the proxy's public port.

DXWEB_CLIENT_HOST and DXWEB_CLIENT_SCHEME are generated according
to the selected mode and scheme. With Nginx, the applications listen
on HTTP over loopback even if public access uses HTTPS.

MOJO_REVERSE_PROXY=0 is for direct access; 1 is for access through
a trusted HTTP proxy. For manual configuration, see section 8.2
regarding headers and the client's real IP address. Do not enable
this trust for direct clients.

DXWEB_TLS_CERT and DXWEB_TLS_KEY are included only when the
applications serve HTTPS directly. With Nginx, the certificate
and key are configured in the proxy, not the HTTP backends.

To check the actual installed values:

  systemctl cat dxweb.service
  systemctl cat dxweb-admin.service

The examples above describe new installations. Existing service
units may retain different local values.

9. LOGS AND DIAGNOSTICS
-----------------------

  journalctl -u dxweb.service -n 100 --no-pager
  journalctl -u dxweb-admin.service -n 100 --no-pager
  systemctl status dxweb.service --no-pager
  systemctl status dxweb-admin.service --no-pager

With Nginx, also check:

  nginx -t
  journalctl -u nginx.service -n 100 --no-pager

If the manager detects an incompatible Nginx site, do not delete
the existing configuration. Plan a migration with a prior backup.
If NO CONFIG CHANGES appears, it may still be necessary to apply
the installation from the menu after updating the Git code.

10. TECHNICAL SYSTEMD EXAMPLES
------------------------------

Four independent examples can be distributed:

  dxweb.service.example
  dxweb-admin.service.example
  dxweb.service.nginx.example
  dxweb-admin.service.nginx.example

They are references for manual testing, NOT installation steps.
The manager generates installed units directly and preserves some
pre-existing local options. Therefore, examples should represent
a new installation using sysop:spider, /spider as the actual path,
and DXS_HOST=127.0.0.1, DXS_PORT=27754. If /spider is a symbolic
link, the manager uses the resolved physical path; examples require
adjusting that path before a manual installation.

The examples will be checked against the unit generator before
being added to the repository. Do not replace an operational unit
with an example without a backup and checking their differences.

11. LIMITATIONS AND SECURITY
----------------------------

HTTPS is recommended whenever DXWeb or DXWeb Admin is accessible
from the Internet or public networks.

This recommendation is especially important for DXWeb Admin,
which provides access to administrative functions and requires
authentication.

Unencrypted HTTP should be reserved for trusted local networks
or controlled test environments.

Nginx support is provided as a configuration example, without
guaranteed compatibility for all scenarios. External proxies,
URL subpaths and certificates must be validated separately.

Do not recursively modify ownership or permissions under
/spider to resolve access problems.

Installation does not require stopping or reinstalling DXSpider.
Do not use this manual to administer other projects on the node.
